                               \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252833 1113 #uuid "92ce441e-2e3a-56bf-bcce-93d7c875b191" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252833 1145 "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Hence, $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(3,\\frac{5}{2})$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(3,\\frac{5}{2})$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3-a}+\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {5}^{3-a} & =1 \\\\ 3-a & =0 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252834 1000 #edb/ref 17592186252835 13194139533810 true] [17592186252834 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533810 true] [17592186252834 1041 "field-1" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252834 1104 #edb/ref 17592186252835 13194139533810 true] [17592186252834 1110 #uuid "4e369fcc-a4b9-54ee-ba63-759925e19a34" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252835 1034 "3" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533810 true] [17592186252835 1111 #uuid "e2e76a45-fe89-5dbf-a58a-d04d1080ba92" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186252837 13194139533810 true] [17592186252836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533810 true] [17592186252836 1041 "field-2" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186252837 13194139533810 true] [17592186252836 1110 #uuid "e3bf525a-d2eb-5abc-99ae-5519f29407bd" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252837 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533810 true] [17592186252837 1111 #uuid "cbd04ec5-c9e4-5dec-b2b4-e9b77f047ae0" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186252839 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1089 "q-167558" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1113 #uuid "f2f8a211-58b3-5945-a90e-d557629feeb1" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252838 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=5+\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186252840 13194139533810 true] [17592186252839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533810 true] [17592186252839 1041 "field-1" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186252840 13194139533810 true] [17592186252839 1110 #uuid "08068434-977d-52eb-843f-8f938ad330f7" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252840 1034 "5+\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533810 true] [17592186252840 1111 #uuid "c3362267-5508-5b69-9b97-e8bb31b1e417" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186252842 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1089 "q-167559" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1113 #uuid "51669125-3606-55fa-9be9-54fcf245279c" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252841 1145 "We have to solve $g(x)=3,$ or equivalently, $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $2\\sqrt{x}$ by adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{1}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4}-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 4-1 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3$  is $x=4.$" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186252843 13194139533810 true] [17592186252842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533810 true] [17592186252842 1041 "field-1" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186252843 13194139533810 true] [17592186252842 1110 #uuid "711754b6-5810-5913-a1d2-33f1d87f386a" 