ion-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWhat is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $4?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,$ thousand dollars per week.\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252862 1113 #uuid "86d53093-c06f-5d6b-b0e0-ca747c543f22" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252862 1145 "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{(\\frac{6}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252863 1000 #edb/ref 17592186252864 13194139533810 true] [17592186252863 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533810 true] [17592186252863 1041 "field-1" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252863 1104 #edb/ref 17592186252864 13194139533810 true] [17592186252863 1110 #uuid "8c453d36-cc71-5235-9d6a-6d62513a3c2f" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252864 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}x^{6/5}" 13194139533810 true] [17592186252864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533810 true] [17592186252864 1111 #uuid "52960447-a4aa-575d-a514-f1086596c54e" 13194139533810 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-167557" 17592186252833 "q-167557/field-1" 17592186252834 "q-167557/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" 17592186252835 "q-167557/field-2" 17592186252836 "q-167557/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" 17592186252837 "q-167558" 17592186252838 "q-167558/field-1" 17592186252839 "q-167558/field-1/6eb084ad90ab80916bda4a9f74ffdf0240dd7c084313f9bc9838389cec8b77ae" 17592186252840 "q-167559" 17592186252841 "q-167559/field-1" 17592186252842 "q-167559/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" 17592186252843 "q-167560" 17592186252844 "q-167560/field-1" 17592186252845 "q-167560/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" 17592186252846 "q-167560/field-2" 17592186252847 "q-167560/field-2/62a6